# Lesson 3, new exercise: lose the first diamond on purpose, so that dummy's long diamonds can all be reached later.
title: Lose the first diamond
id: L03-18
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2, 3.4, 3.5
student: S
board: 50
dealer: E
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  J64    J32    AK7542  Q
  E  732    Q865   JT9     T96
  S  AK5    AK4    63      J8752
  W  QT98   T97    Q8      AK43

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 15 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 6-9
  spades: 2 = The ace and king.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 2-5 = Two on top. Five if they split 3-2 and you give up the first round, to keep a way over to dummy.
  clubs: 0 = None.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: Dummy's diamonds could give five tricks, but dummy has no other way in. How can you keep a diamond to lead over to dummy?
play:
  1   W CA  N CQ  E C6  S C2
      note: West leads the top clubs.
  2   W CK  N H2  E C9  S C5
  3   W HT  N H3  E H5  S HA
      note: Six winners on top: two in each of spades, hearts and diamonds. Dummy's long diamonds can give three more, if the opponents' five diamonds split 3-2. But dummy has no way in except diamonds. So give up the first diamond, and keep one in your hand to lead over to dummy later.
  4   S D3  W D8  N D2*  E D9
      note: Let the opponents win this diamond. You still have a diamond left to lead over to dummy next time.
      D2 key: Duck. You have to lose a diamond anyway, so lose it now, while you still have a diamond to lead to dummy.
      D2 not DA: Did you think about how to get back to dummy? If you play the ace and king, the third diamond is lost, and dummy has no other way in to its last diamonds.
      D2 not DK: Did you think about how to get back to dummy? If you play the ace and king, the third diamond is lost, and dummy has no other way in to its last diamonds.
  5   E S7  S SK  W S8  N S4
  6   S D6  W DQ  N DA  E DT
      note: Now lead your last diamond over to dummy.
  7   N DK  E DJ  S C7  W S9
      note: The opponents' diamonds are all gone. Dummy's are all winners.
  8   N D7  E S2  S H4  W C3
      note: Keep cashing dummy's diamonds.
  9   N D5  E S3  S S5  W C4
      note: Another diamond.
  10  N D4  E H6  S C8  W H7
      note: The last diamond.
  11  N SJ  E H8  S SA  W ST
      note: Back to your hand with the ♠A.
  12  S HK  W H9  N HJ  E HQ
      note: The ♥K.
  13  S CJ  W SQ  N S6  E CT
      note: Your ♣J is the last club left. 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: count six winners on top, then set up dummy's diamonds by giving
  up the first round, while you still had a diamond to lead to dummy.

  If you had played the ace and king first, the third round would have
  been lost, and you would have had no way back to dummy's last three
  diamonds.
